      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Presence snapshot endpoint leaks any user's online status to any authenticated caller, cross-tenant, with no relationship check.\n\nThe `userIds` query parameter is fully client-controlled and is used directly as the lookup key set into the global, non-tenant-scoped `IPresenceTracker` singleton. The only gate is `.RequireAuthorization()` (any authenticated user of the platform) — there is no check that the caller shares a tenant, channel, or any other relationship with the target user ids before returning their online/offline status. Also CWE-862: GET /api/v1/realtime/presence takes an attacker-controlled, comma-separated list of arbitrary userIds from the query string and returns each one's online/offline status straight from the process-wide IPresenceTracker singleton. The only guard is `.RequireAuthorization()` (any authenticated user of any tenant) — there is no check that the caller shares a tenant, channel, or any other relationship with the requested userIds.\n\nImpact: Any authenticated user (from any tenant, since the tracker has no tenant partitioning) can enumerate the real-time online/offline status of arbitrary user GUIDs across the whole deployment — a user-enumeration / presence-leak information-disclosure primitive that the hub's own broadcast path explicitly avoids (AppHub scopes PresenceChanged broadcasts to the tenant group, see AppHub.cs:93-123), making this endpoint inconsistent with the component's own tenant-isolation intent.\n\nExploit scenario: A low-privileged user in Tenant A authenticates, obtains a JWT, then calls GET /api/v1/realtime/presence?userIds=<guid-from-tenant-B>,<guid-from-tenant-B-2> and receives each target's live online/offline status, even though they have no membership or relationship with Tenant B or its users.\n\nPreconditions: Caller must be authenticated (any valid JWT for the deployment, any tenant/role); Caller must know or guess target user GUIDs (not enumerable from this endpoint alone, but often discoverable via other IDOR-prone endpoints, shared channels, or ticket references elsewhere in the app).\n\nRecommendation: Scope the presence lookup to users the caller is actually allowed to see (same tenant and/or shared channel membership) before querying IPresenceTracker, mirroring the tenant-group scoping already applied to the hub's PresenceChanged broadcast. Also CWE-862: Scope the lookup to the caller's own tenant (and ideally to users the caller actually shares a channel/relationship with), mirroring the tenant-scoped group broadcast already done in AppHub.OnConnectedAsync, instead of querying the global PresenceTracker by raw user id.\n\nCWE-639. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
