        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-362",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "SSE token consume-and-delete is not atomic, letting a single-use stream token authenticate more than one concurrent stream.\n\nConsumeAsync is the sole authorization check for the AllowAnonymous /api/v1/sse/stream endpoint. It reads the token payload from the distributed cache, then deletes it in a separate, non-atomic step (cache.RemoveAsync at line 49). Two requests presenting the same token concurrently can both read the payload before either delete completes, both passing the authorization gate.\n\nImpact: Weakens the single-use guarantee: a token that leaks (it travels in the query string per the endpoint's own design, so it is exposed to proxy/server logs, browser history and Referer headers) can authenticate two or more concurrent SSE streams instead of exactly one, extending the usable window for a leaked token beyond the intended single consumption.\n\nExploit scenario: A token leaks via a server/proxy access log entry shortly after issuance. Before the legitimate client's single consumption completes its RemoveAsync call, an attacker who scraped the log replays the same token and both the legitimate client and the attacker establish live SSE streams receiving the same user's real-time events.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker (or a second party) must already possess a valid, unexpired (30s) SSE token, typically via leakage through logs/Referer/history since the token is query-string-borne by design; Requests must race within the same ~tens-of-milliseconds window before the first consumer's RemoveAsync completes.\n\nRecommendation: Make consumption atomic, e.g. use an atomic get-and-delete primitive (Redis GETDEL) or a distributed lock/compare-and-swap so only the first reader of a given token can ever obtain the payload.\n\nCWE-362. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
